utting-concerns.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Test evidence is stale and belongs to another branch's tree", "recommendation": "Instance. `prawduct-hook test-status` exits 1 'stale'. The recorded evidence (`evidence_tree` a235c57a, timestamp 2026-10-27T22:56:56Z, changes_referenced all unloaded-tripwire files) is for commit cc480c37 on feature/1078-unloaded-tripwire, which is not an ancestor of HEAD. Its one failure (tests.test_api_contract_json::test_every_json_subcommand_documents_its_key_set) is that tree's, not this one's: this diff adds no --json subcommand. So nothing here shows that this branch's suite passes (head_tree 663c33e2), and that includes the 50+ new case-table tests and the renegotiated CLAUSE test. Run the declared suite through `test-evidence record` on this tree before the merge.", "files": ["tests/test_chunk_state_table.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "plan_lifecycle: a newer dirty-tree fact at the same anchor now hides older legitimate merge evidence (fast-forward regression)", "recommendation": "Instance. `_anchors_newest_first` used to skip a fact whose anchor equals its own `base_commit` without adding that anchor to `seen`. It now adds the fact, flagged, and dedupes by anchor alone. Take a committed cumulative R1 anchored at X (base_commit Y), then a later dirty-tree verify-resolutions V1 dispatched at X (anchor X == base_commit X), then a fast-forward merge with the branch deleted. Before this change R1 proved the merge. Now V1 claims X first, R1 is dropped as a duplicate, `on_first_parent(X, base)` is true after the fast-forward, and the anchor is refused. `_merge_evidence` then falls through to the pending-change-record inference or to `unknown`. A no-ff merge is unaffected. Fix: dedupe on (anchor, is_base_commit), or let a non-base_commit fact replace a flagged one for the same anchor. Add a lifecycle test for this fast-forward shape. The current new test covers only no-ff.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py", "tests/test_plan_lifecycle.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "The new fail-closed git-failure branches and the first-parent probe's cost are unpinned", "recommendation": "Class: every git-failure branch this diff adds. Search plan_status.py and plan_lifecycle.py for `rc != 0` and for the `None` returns that `_Spans._log` / `on_first_parent` feed. Three branches have no test. (1) `on_first_parent` answers True, which refuses, on a rev-list failure or empty output (its docstring: 'Git failing answers yes, which refuses: authority fails closed'). (2) `_Spans.carried` returns nothing when `_log` fails ('whose interval git cannot list, carries nothing'). (3) The dirty-tree span gives up when `holder` is None. All three are in the authority path, and a regression there turns into a false 'done'. Separately, the plan_status and plan_lifecycle NF1 docstrings say the new first-parent `rev-list` (and, for a dirty-tree span, the second `--ancestry-path` log) are 'pinned by call/spy'. No spy test asserts either call: the one new cost test covers a committed span only. Add a monkeypatched-git-failure test for each branch, plus spy assertions for the rev-list and the dirty-tree log.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py", "tests/test_plan_lifecycle.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "note", "title": "Span cost statement omits the per-fragment git show", "recommendation": "Instance; no edit wanted. `_Spans.carried` calls `_work_paths(..., _read_at(repo, head_tree))`. That runs one `git show` per change-fragment path in the interval, and it is not memoized across spanning facts. The `_Spans` docstring ('One git log per distinct interval') and the module's NF1 Cost paragraph do not count it. The figure is inert: no gate reads it, and the count is bounded by fragments in one interval. It matters only if someone tightens the Stop-path spy test to match the docstring.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Span certification has its own copy of the commit-to-chunk rule and the git-log parser", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `_Spans` re-implements two things plan_status already had. (1) `_Spans._log` parses the same `_COMMIT_MARK` + `--name-only` log format that `_walk_branch` parses, with only `%H %T %P` added. (2) `_Spans.carried` re-composes the certifying rule (scoped subject via `_carries_scope` + `_named_ids`, Deliverables touch via `_touches`, minus stub fragments via `_work_paths`/`_read_at`), which `chunk_proposal` and `built_report` already compose, each in its own way. The `_Spans` docstring says it uses 'the proposal's certifying rule (chunk_proposal)', and the plan's case for span-done is that the instruments cannot disagree. Today that agreement comes from a parallel copy, not from a shared owner: the next change to the proposal's rule (scope fallback, the C6 early-touch invariant, the stub rule) reaches `chunk_proposal` and silently misses the span, and the span certifies with no later review. Fix: extract one log reader returning `_Commit` records, used by both `_walk_branch` and `_Spans`, and one `chunks carried by these commits` predicate that `chunk_proposal`'s subject half and `_Spans.carried` both call.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "Prose still says --chunks is the certified set, but a span now certifies alongside it", "recommendation": "Scope: class. The bounded set is any sentence that equates the named or proposed chunk set with what a review certifies. To find more members, search plugin/ for `--chunks` and 'certif'. Under case C4, a fact naming 01 whose span carries 02 certifies both, so naming no longer narrows. These sentences still say otherwise: (a) plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md step 4, '**`--chunks` names the chunks this review certifies**'. This diff edited the next sentence of that step and left this one. A dispatcher who reads it will pass a narrower `--chunks` expecting to hold a chunk back, and it gets certified anyway. Across skills it contradicts planning.md's new 'Chunk state' definition, which is a Framework Check 8 (Instruction Clarity) contradiction. (b) plugin/skills/pr/review-protocol.md:87, 'the usual cause is a review dispatched without it in `--chunks`', which is no longer the usual cause of an open delivered chunk once a cumulative review's span certifies it. (c) the `chunk_proposal` docstring in plan_status.py, 'the proposal is what the fact certifies when the builder declares nothing'. The remedy is relational wording: `--chunks` names chunks, and certification is the named set plus the span, per planning.md 'Chunk state'. Point at that one home and do not restate the rule.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "on_first_parent is a git primitive in plan_status, reached by a lazy import whose stated reason is false", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `on_first_parent(repo_dir, anchor, base)` is a pure git-history question with no plan state. It lives in plan_status, and plan_lifecycle reaches it through a function-local `from .plan_status import on_first_parent  # plan_status reaches this module lazily`. plan_status imports plan_lifecycle only under `TYPE_CHECKING`, and no runtime path from plan_status reaches plan_lifecycle, so a top-level import would not form a cycle and the comment states a reason that does not exist. Moving the function to gitstate (or evidence, beside `run_git`) gives the one rule an owner that both modules already import, and the lazy import and its comment go away. This is comment wording plus placement: nothing misbehaves today, and no edit is required.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "_Reachability.holder is a side channel valid only after an accepting __call__", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `holder(anchor)` returns whatever `_first_held` or the base path stored as a side effect of an earlier `__call__`. Its only caller, the span loop in `chunk_states`, keeps that order correctly. Still, the contract 'call reachable(...) first and get None, then ask holder' is temporal coupling that the type does not express. Returning the holding ref from the accepting path, for example a small result carrying the reason or the ref, would remove it. It is borderline because it is localized to one caller, and no edit is required.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "blocking", "title": "Owner ruling D1 is extended to span-derived done with no owner confirmation on record outside this branch", "recommendation": "Core learning 'A governance change cannot supply its own authority: when an agent amends a binding norm mid-build, land the owner's confirmation somewhere the amendment isn't' fires here. plan_status.chunk_states now certifies chunks a review fact does not name (_Spans). That changes the owner's D1 ruling in data-model.md section Direction ('A chunk is done when a review fact names it ...'). The only authority it cites is 'the coordinator's design decision for #1082, owner request for 3.8.0', and both live in this branch's own plan. The owner asked for #1079 and #1082 to land in 3.8.0, but did not choose option 1 at #1082's open fork. The plan itself says 'the owner's confirmation of option 1 lives with the coordinator, not here', and the gitignored handoff still lists it as owed: 'Owner to confirm: option 1 ... the confirmation must land outside this branch, e.g. a comment on #1082', together with the M5/M6 fail-closed outcomes. Fix (instance): get the owner's confirm-or-correct on option 1 and on M5/M6 somewhere the amendment is not (e.g. a comment on #1082), then cite that in the D1 extension note instead of 'owner request for 3.8.0'. If the owner corrects it, the code changes, not the ruling. In the same confirmation, settle the Goals' '*Inferred:* \"the chunk's built commit\" is ... the existing _walk_branch / built_report walk'. The build replaced that inference with the fact's own interval (mid-build decision), but the Goal is still marked inferred.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-state-table.md"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Records Pass", "severity": "warning", "title": "Change record's consumer note ships a wrong remedy for fast-forward and squash merges, and claims the briefing reads the lifecycle", "recommendation": "Bars: it ships (the change fragment becomes the release note) and it misleads into action. The consumer note says 'Chunks merged by fast-forward or squash with no review naming them still read open after the branch is deleted. Pass `--chunks` on the boundary review if that is how you merge.' The case table says otherwise. M6 (test_M6_squash_merge_branch_deleted_is_open) reads open with a fact that NAMES chunk 01 (chunks=[\"01\"]), because the squash leaves the reviewed anchor on no ref. M5 (test_M5_...) reads open with a named dirty-tree fact. So naming does not rescue either case, and a squash-merging consumer who follows the advice still gets open chunks and `unbuilt_at_archive` stamps. Separately, the Chunk 03 bullet says 'plan status's lifecycle line, plan check-claims and the briefing also read #1079's case as merged-unreleased'. Nothing under plugin/hooks or plugin/lib calls plan_lifecycle.observe for the briefing; only `plan status` and `plan check-claims` in plugin/bin/prawduct-hook do, and #1106 is the separate branch that would add the briefing. Fix (instance): restate the M5/M6 limitation as the table derives it (fast-forward with a dirty-tree review anchored at its base_commit, and any squash merge, read open after the branch is deleted, whether or not the review named the chunk; keep the branch until release, or merge with a merge commit). Drop 'and the briefing'. Per authoring learning 'A claim copied from a record ... re-derive it from code, git or the source before propagating it', the note's wording came from the handoff's summary, not from the tests.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/chunk-state-table.md"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "A span refused for an observable reason leaves the chunk plain open with no reason, and the NEXT-ACTION clause then misdescribes it", "recommendation": "Named facts that cannot certify record a reason via note(). The span path does not. A spanning fact that is not an edge (C8), whose dirty tree was edited before commit (C11), that has no base_commit (C12), or whose `git log` fails (_Spans._log returns None) leaves the chunk open with reason None. The ChunkState docstring says an open chunk's reason explains 'why something that looked like an answer was not one'. In those cases critic_consolidate.uncertified_clause then says 'neither its proposal nor its interval found work for them (no commit subject in it names them ...)', which is false when a scoped commit does name the chunk. The remedy it prints (--chunks) still works, so nobody takes a wrong action. Only the diagnosis is misleading. Scope: class, covering every early return in _Spans.carried and the edge filter in chunk_states. No edit is required.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions for the owner", "recommendation": "Scope: none. The plan's 'Decisions made mid-build' records these, all coordinator or delegate calls with no owner settlement on record: (1) option 1, span-derived done, chosen at #1082's fork; (2) 'built commit' means a commit in the fact's own interval, attributed by the proposal's certifying rule, replacing the Goals' inferred whole-branch walk; (3) a span certifies only through a coverage_algebra edge; (4) a dirty-tree fact's uncommitted part counts only once committed verbatim, and a fact with no base_commit spans nothing; (5) M5 (fast-forward, anchor = base_commit) and M6 (squash) stay open, failing closed; (6) the #1079 rule refuses an anchor equal to its base_commit only on the base's first-parent history; (7) naming answers before span, and a span-done carries a reason; (8) plan retract can withdraw a span certification only while it holds; (9) D1 gets a dated extension note rather than a rewrite; (10) Chunk 03 widened into plan_lifecycle and critic_consolidate so one owner (plan_status.on_first_parent) serves both. Alignment-pass miss: the plan proceeded on 'owner request for 3.8.0' while #1082 had an open design fork. An ask-in-one-batch for option 1 at plan time would have settled the blocking finding above before the build.", "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "Backlog Reconciliation", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: brookstalley/prawduct#1079 (dirty-tree chunk review reads open after its plan merges)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Covered by M2/M3 and the plan_lifecycle test. Verify it, then call `/prawduct:backlog update brookstalley/prawduct#1079 status=shipped closed-by=scope:chunk-state-table` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says. The change record's closes: already lists it.", "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Backlog Reconciliation", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: brookstalley/prawduct#1082 (a chunk covered by an unnamed review stays open)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Covered by C13/C14. Verify it, then call `/prawduct:backlog update brookstalley/prawduct#1082 status=shipped closed-by=scope:chunk-state-table` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says. The option-1 confirmation in the blocking finding should land before this one closes.", "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "Backlog Reconciliation", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: brookstalley/prawduct#1092 (chunk state derived from review facts keeps regressing)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. The case table plus one test per row is #1092's ask. Verify it, then call `/prawduct:backlog update brookstalley/prawduct#1092 status=shipped closed-by=scope:chunk-state-table` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says.", "fid": "R-15"}, {"goal": "Backlog Reconciliation", "severity": "note", "title": "C-B3: open item brookstalley/prawduct#672 affects plan_status/critic_consolidate and was not assessed", "recommendation": "Scope: none. `cache-query affecting` returns #672 ('coverage composes by tree but its gates key on identity') for the changed files. This change makes chunk state compose over the same tree span the coverage gate uses, which may narrow #672. Assess it and update its status or notes. #928 also matched by path but is unrelated (host adapters).", "fid": "R-16"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 6, "note": 9}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 720, "scope": "chunk-state-table", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02", "03"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02", "03"], "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "eb4de60ea330e0d9cfc8e1a40665936e76b2d2ad", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-state-table.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-state-table.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "